Jesse Frizzell,

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.234
it is a Kubernetes
native project.

00:02:24.234 --> 00:02:26.150
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Like,
Cloud Native, but nice,

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:26.840
Kubernetes Native.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:28.190
MARK MANDEL: It's
Kubernetes Native.

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:29.150
I super really like it.

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:31.640
And at top level, it
basically gives you

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:34.476
visibility and control over
your microservices traffic.

00:02:34.476 --> 00:02:36.350
So if you've ever had
a million microservices

00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:38.060
and you're like, which
one's talking to which,

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:40.309
what's their status, what
are they doing, how are they

00:02:40.309 --> 00:02:42.440
working, can I prevent
other microservices

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:44.870
connecting to other ones, like
doing security and control.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:45.595
All those things.

00:02:45.595 --> 00:02:47.970
That's the problem that istio
is trying to solve for you.

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:48.980
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
I was always saying,

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.590
the cool thing is that you
don't need to modify anything.

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.650
The way this works is by
running a sidecar container

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:55.760
in your pod.

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:58.370
So that way, you are able
to understand everything

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.290
that goes through the network.

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.990
And it uses Zipkin,
and you can actually

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.330
plug it to things like
Prometheus and Grafana,

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.430
to actually understand
what's going on.

