But can I do this also
with local source code,

00:09:12.100 --> 00:09:15.190
and get a local Docker
image, but with doing

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:19.000
all the build steps that
could be expensive somehow?

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:21.334
Doing that on the cloud, is
that also part of the thing?

00:09:21.334 --> 00:09:23.250
DAVID BENDORY: Sure, so
we're fully integrated

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:24.260
with the Cloud SDK.

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:27.280
So from your gcloud command
line that you, no doubt, know

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:31.360
and love, you can just do a
gcloud container build submit

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:34.840
and take your local source,
which, presumably, hasn't yet

00:09:34.840 --> 00:09:36.910
been submitted to a
source code repository,

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:38.380
you're just playing
around locally.

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:40.840
And you can submit that
build and know exactly how it

00:09:40.840 --> 00:09:45.120
will build when you actually
do commit the source and upload

00:09:45.120 --> 00:09:46.630
it to your repository somewhere.

00:09:46.630 --> 00:09:49.300
MARK MANDEL: OK cool, is
that taking it through, say,

00:09:49.300 --> 00:09:50.650
some automated build steps?

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.360
Or is that just
building, say, a Docker

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:54.280
file that I'm pushing up?

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:55.780
DAVID BENDORY: It
could be either.

00:09:55.780 --> 00:09:58.600
So in the simplest use case,
you've got a Docker file

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:01.060
and you can use the correct
flags on the command line

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.520
to just pass that Docker
file and some source in,

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.990
and we'll just do it simple
Docker build on that.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:08.090
As you get more
sophisticated, you probably

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:11.300
switch from a simple Docker
file to a configuration file,

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:14.300
we call it cloudbuild.yaml,
and that effectively

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:16.100
specifies a series
of build steps

00:10:16.100 --> 00:10:19.850
that you want to run in order
to make your build happen.

00:10:19.850 --> 00:10:22.280
And we will then execute
that build for you.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:23.930
FRANSESC CAMPOY:
Those steps, I can

00:10:23.930 --> 00:10:28.340
imagine those steps being kind
of similar to what you can

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:32.120
do with, let's say, Travis CI?

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.070
So is this a
continuous integration

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:36.950
tool, continuous
delivery, continues

00:10:36.950 --> 00:10:39.700
deployment, continuous
[INAUDIBLE]??

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:41.880
CHRISTOPHER SANSON: That's
a very good question.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:43.880
It's whatever people
want it to be.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:48.050
So the idea is, it's not
a full CI or CD solution,

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:50.900
but the build step
architecture is very flexible.

00:10:50.900 --> 00:10:54.710
Essentially, we run a container
from an image that you specify.

00:10:54.710 --> 00:10:56.990
And so we have some
supportive build steps,

00:10:56.990 --> 00:10:58.430
so Docker is a
good example, where

00:10:58.430 --> 00:10:59.760
you can do a Docker build step.

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:03.000
But these images are--
it's bring your own image.

00:11:03.000 --> 00:11:06.050
So users can define whatever
they want as a container that

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:07.160
can execute.

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:10.190
And so, for example, you
can run a gcloud command,

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:12.310
you could run a Firebase deploy.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:15.830
Any tool that has a CLI you
can turn into a build step.

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:17.910
And people do use it
for testing as well.

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:21.710
So you could, conceivably, set
up a sort of a full solution,

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:24.980
where you trigger changes on
source, you run your tests,

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:26.990
you build your
artifact, you maybe

00:11:26.990 --> 00:11:30.710
run some integration tests,
and then create your binary,

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:31.880
and then deploy it out.

00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:33.800
It's kind of a little
bit DIY at this point,

00:11:33.800 --> 00:11:35.240
in terms of using it that way.

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:37.520
I mean, the real
core use case is

00:11:37.520 --> 00:11:40.250
you're ready to create
your release candidate, you

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:43.040
merge your code into
master, trigger a build,

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:44.360
and then push that out.

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:45.540
But, yeah, I mean it's
really interesting.

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.540
We're seeing people use
it in all sorts of ways.

00:11:47.540 --> 00:11:51.780
They're creating Maven
builders, Gradle builders, Gulp,

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:52.810
sort of you name it.

00:11:52.810 --> 00:11:54.310
DAVID BENDORY: So
these build steps,

00:11:54.310 --> 00:11:57.110
the idea here is flexibility.

00:11:57.110 --> 00:11:59.810
The way we solve the
problem of you not knowing

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:02.780
what the build chain is
going to be in the cloud

00:12:02.780 --> 00:12:05.000
is by letting you bring
your own build chain.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:08.030
So if you can put it
into a Docker container,

00:12:08.030 --> 00:12:10.100
then we can execute
it as a build step.

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:13.370
So effectively, for example,
the standard Maven container

00:12:13.370 --> 00:12:16.730
that's available on Docker hub,
can be executed as a build step

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:18.370
in Container Builder.

00:12:18.370 --> 00:12:21.740
And we are seeing now our
more sophisticated users

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:23.240
develop their own
build steps, to do

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:25.656
whatever custom work that they
want to do as part of their

00:12:25.656 --> 00:12:26.360
build.

00:12:26.360 --> 00:12:28.370
And we are watching
the development

00:12:28.370 --> 00:12:31.310
of a sort of ecosystem of
different kinds of builders

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:33.740
that are language
specific, some of which

00:12:33.740 --> 00:12:36.880
we're going to end up officially
supporting, some of which

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:39.110
won't be officially
supported by our team,

00:12:39.110 --> 00:12:40.850
but might exist
in a solution dock

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:44.000
or some such, that will
enable people to share

00:12:44.000 --> 00:12:45.309
their ideas in this regard.

00:12:45.309 --> 00:12:47.100
MARK MANDEL: So then,
that's exactly what I

00:12:47.100 --> 00:12:47.970
was going to ask.

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:50.750
Is there some sort of set of
examples people should go look

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:52.269
at, or community
work that you've

00:12:52.269 --> 00:12:54.560
seen that's out in the wild,
that people should go hunt

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.810
down and check out what's going
on with Container Builder?

00:12:56.810 --> 00:12:57.643
DAVID BENDORY: Sure.

00:12:57.643 --> 00:13:00.390
Well from our docks, we link
to our GitHub repository,

00:13:00.390 --> 00:13:02.750
where we've open sourced
about a half a dozen

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:05.960
build steps that we officially
support, coming out of my team.

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.502
And those are
things like Docker,

00:13:07.502 --> 00:13:08.960
that Christopher
already mentioned,

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:10.790
we've got a Bazel
build step, we've

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:13.820
got golanguage builders
there, a GIF builder,

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:17.190
so you can pull from
get or potentially

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:22.260
push to get as part of your
build if you want to do that.

00:13:22.260 --> 00:13:26.429
And so in each of those build
steps, we provide examples,

00:13:26.429 --> 00:13:28.220
and people can see
exactly what we've done,

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:29.600
it's all open source.

00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:31.850
And we also have sort
of little demos there

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:33.770
and examples of how
to use them, in order

00:13:33.770 --> 00:13:36.020
to encourage this kind
of experimentation

00:13:36.020 --> 00:13:36.880
among our users.

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:37.796
FRANSESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:37.796 --> 00:13:39.710
So one of the
things that I think

00:13:39.710 --> 00:13:44.300
is an amazing advantage of
using Container Builder is

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:46.950
that since it runs on
Google Cloud Platform,

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:51.470
I'm assuming integration with
other Google Cloud Platform

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:53.960
services, especially
authentication,

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:55.410
is already managed?

00:13:55.410 --> 00:13:56.600
Am I correct?

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:59.900
What if I want to access the
metadata servers to download

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:02.390
some metadata that
I'm going to use

00:14:02.390 --> 00:14:05.660
to access some private GitHub
repo or something like that?

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:09.014
Is that the case, that you just
do get and that will work, or?

00:14:09.014 --> 00:14:10.680
DAVID BENDORY: So for
metadata services,

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:12.960
we've wired that access
into place for you.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:16.310
We also support the
gsutil and a gcloud

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:18.710
build steps, one of each.

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:22.370
That will let you just run
command lines as part of your

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:23.450
build.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:26.180
The authorization story is a
little bit complex right now,

00:14:26.180 --> 00:14:28.990
we're in the process of
simplifying that for our users.

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:31.370
Where we're going to
land in the coming weeks

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:35.840
is that you will be able to set
up build permissions using IAM,

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:38.330
the same way you set up roles
and permissions for anything

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:39.440
else on your project.

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:42.440
So you'll effectively be
able to enable your Cloud

00:14:42.440 --> 00:14:45.141
Builder to do whatever it
is that you want it to do.

00:14:45.141 --> 00:14:45.890
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:47.510
So let's sort of
talk practically.

00:14:47.510 --> 00:14:51.545
Say I want to hook up a
Container Builder to maybe

00:14:51.545 --> 00:14:55.820
a GitHub repo or a Source repo
and have it build a Docker

00:14:55.820 --> 00:14:57.650
container at the end of it.

00:14:57.650 --> 00:15:00.200
What does that developer
process look like?

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:02.158
DAVID BENDORY: So the
way that would work today

00:15:02.158 --> 00:15:05.000
is you would go into our
UI in the cloud console

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:09.440
and you would follow a
series of steps there

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:12.660
that first set up repo
sync from your private--

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:14.660
I assume you're talking
about a private GitHub--

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:18.440
so from your private GitHub
to Cloud Source Repositories.

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:22.010
You could then set
up triggers on that,

00:15:22.010 --> 00:15:25.312
so that whenever there is a
push to the branch or branches

00:15:25.312 --> 00:15:27.270
you want, that will
trigger an automatic build,

00:15:27.270 --> 00:15:29.420
which will run almost
immediately when

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:31.370
you push a change.

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:34.550
And then we have a lot
of users doing things

00:15:34.550 --> 00:15:38.765
like listening to GCR
Pub/Sub event notifications

00:15:38.765 --> 00:15:41.360
so that they know then a
new container gets pushed.

00:15:41.360 --> 00:15:43.280
And when that new
container gets pushed,

00:15:43.280 --> 00:15:46.490
that triggers some other step
in a continuous integration

00:15:46.490 --> 00:15:49.940
or continuous delivery pipeline
that they want to run on Push.

00:15:49.940 --> 00:15:52.490
You can also listen for Pub/Sub
notifications on your builds

00:15:52.490 --> 00:15:55.740
if you want, but that will be
a little bit noisier than just

00:15:55.740 --> 00:15:56.950
the container push.

00:15:56.950 --> 00:15:58.730
You'll get notifications
about builds

00:15:58.730 --> 00:16:02.030
when they start working,
you'll get build completion

00:16:02.030 --> 00:16:04.330
notifications, whether it's
success or failure, which

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:05.900
is a little bit different
than just listening for what

00:16:05.900 --> 00:16:08.275
a new container shows up,
which obviously indicates build

00:16:08.275 --> 00:16:08.930
success.

00:16:08.930 --> 00:16:10.304
FRANSESC CAMPOY:
So you mentioned

00:16:10.304 --> 00:16:13.430
that the first step is to go
to the Container Builder tab

00:16:13.430 --> 00:16:14.570
on the console.

00:16:14.570 --> 00:16:16.150
I cannot find it.

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:16.734
Where is that?

00:16:16.734 --> 00:16:17.900
DAVID BENDORY: You're Right.

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:20.630
I don't think I specifically
said the Container Builder tab,

00:16:20.630 --> 00:16:22.141
but I should have
been more clear.

00:16:22.141 --> 00:16:24.640
So everything I just described
is under the Google Container

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:25.900
registry.

00:16:25.900 --> 00:16:28.220
FRANSESC CAMPOY: OK, that
makes a lot of sense.

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:29.530
Good.

00:16:29.530 --> 00:16:31.370
I was like, I'm trying
to follow through,

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:32.930
I was like, I'm lost now.

00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:33.950
DAVID BENDORY: I'm glad
you tried to follow

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:36.660
and asked, because a lot of the
